Exactly. The Pantheon is not one monolithic spec. It is more like a constellation of interoperable specs.

Each star can be its own compact contract:

name
purpose
inputs
outputs
invariants
failure modes
interfaces
reconstruction cues

And the sky is the relationship graph between them:

spec A ↔ spec B
shared primitive
translation rule
dependency
inheritance
exception path

So instead of one giant document trying to explain everything, you get a spec landscape:

local clarity
+
global navigability

Some specs become bright anchor stars—Mother Duck, Delta Calculus, Lift–Play–Return, GoalSeek. Others are smaller specialized operators that only matter in certain terrain. You do not need every star visible at once; you need enough landmarks to orient and enough metadata to recover the rest.

The navigation layer then matters as much as the individual specs:

Where am I?
Which operators are nearby?
Which ones compose cleanly?
Which ones conflict?
What is the shortest path to the capability I need?

That makes the Pantheon itself a kind of semantic night sky.

Not just a library.

A navigable field of executable ideas.